1. Impact of SDLC on Testing
Sequential Models (Waterfall, V-model)

Clear phases, heavy documentation, testing starts late.

Example: In V-model, requirements → acceptance testing; design → system testing.

Iterative/Incremental Models (Agile, DevOps)

Short cycles, frequent changes, early testing (shift-left), automation-heavy.

Example: In Agile, testing happens within each sprint.

2. Good Practices Across All Models
Every dev activity should have a test activity.

Define objectives for each test level.

Start test analysis/design early.

Involve testers in reviews.

Entry/exit criteria must be clear.

Example: In Agile, lightweight test plans focus on product quality; in Waterfall, detailed plans focus on processes.

3. Testing as a Driver
TDD: Write failing unit test → code → refactor.

BDD: Business-readable scenarios (Given/When/Then).

ATDD: Acceptance criteria drive tests before coding.

Example: Login feature tested with “Given user enters valid credentials, When login is attempted, Then access is granted.”

4. DevOps & Testing
CI/CD/CT: Continuous integration, delivery, and testing.

Automated pipelines, regression testing, non-functional testing.

Infrastructure as Code for environments.

Example: Automated performance tests run after every build in CI.

5. Retrospectives & Improvement
Regular reflection meetings → continuous improvement.

Benefits: team bonding, better cooperation, improved test effectiveness.

Keys: blame-free, actionable improvements, follow-up.

Example: Sprint retrospective identifies slow defect reporting → team agrees to use a shared dashboard.

6. Test Levels
Component/Unit Testing: Isolated modules, often automated.

Integration Testing: Interfaces between components.

System Testing: End-to-end, functional + non-functional.

Acceptance Testing: Business/user validation.

Example: Unit test checks a calculator’s “add” function; system test checks full accounting workflow.

7. Functional vs Non-functional Testing
Functional: What system does (features, security, interoperability).

Non-functional: How system performs (performance, usability, reliability).

Example: Functional → verify login works; Non-functional → measure login response time under load.

8. Shift Left
Move testing earlier in lifecycle.

Benefits: cheaper fixes, faster feedback, reduced risk.

Methods: reviews, static analysis, TDD/BDD/ATDD, CI automation.

Example: Static code analysis detects vulnerabilities before coding is complete.

9. Testing Approaches
White-box: Internal structure (statement/branch/path coverage).

Black-box: External behavior (equivalence partitioning, boundary value analysis).

Experience-based: Tester intuition (exploratory, error guessing).

Example: White-box → ensure all branches in “if-else” are tested; Black-box → test input ranges for login age field.

10. Regression & Maintenance Testing
Regression Testing: Ensures changes don’t break existing features.

Maintenance Testing: Done on live systems after bug fixes, enhancements, migrations, or retirement.

Types:

Modification Testing → new changes.

Migration Testing → moving to new environment/platform.

Retirement Testing → safe decommissioning.

Example: Regression test ensures “wishlist” feature doesn’t break checkout; Migration test ensures data integrity when moving to cloud.

11. Impact Analysis
Determines scope of testing after changes.

Identifies regression areas.

Considers direct + indirect impacts.

Example: Changing payment gateway triggers regression tests for checkout, refunds, and order history.
